For any small $\epsilon$, $1+\epsilon\simeq e^\epsilon,$ because $e^\epsilon
=1+\epsilon+\frac{\epsilon^2}{2!}
+\frac{\epsilon^3}{3!}+\cdots.$ Thus, in these temporary units, $1-iA_i$ approximates $e^{-iA_i}$ for a sufficiently small cell. It is cleaner to use the exact exponential factor from the beginning.

